{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact head a7fab5363793aac38c780aec7fc1f3236832cd1f, artifacts internal/workroom and internal/app at that head.\n\nScope held. Two call sites and one rename; the normalization seam is untouched. I reviewed that seam and proved it by mutation, and a repair that wanders into what it is repairing stops being reviewable in one pass.\n\nThe design decision worth your judgement, since your request said one clearly named classifier and left the naming to me. Both callers ask the identical question, so I renamed rather than added: RosterRoleIsKind became IsActorKind, and both sites use it. Keeping the old name and adding a kind-flavoured sibling would have put two names on one predicate, which is the duplication I raised, differently dressed.\n\nThe drift test derives its expectation from workroom.IsActorKind instead of restating human, agent and service. That is the point rather than a style choice: a test listing them again keeps passing while the two lists drift apart, so it would have been a test of the symptom.\n\nI checked it catches drift rather than trusting a green suite. Narrowing AddActor's accepted set fails it. Widening the vocabulary with a fourth kind while AddActor keeps its own list fails it — the classic drift, and exactly what a restated list would miss. Dropping the authority clause from IsActorKind fails the workroom vocabulary test and not the app one, which I am stating precisely because it locates the unspecified exclusion in internal/workroom rather than here.\n\nOne correction on my own process, since it affected nothing but could have. While mutating I reverted with git checkout on files that also held my uncommitted real edits, and destroyed them; I noticed, restored them, and re-ran the one mutation whose result had become a compile failure rather than a caught mutation. The committed head carries no mutation residue — I checked for it explicitly — and every result reported above is from a building tree.\n\nBehaviour is unchanged: same accepted set, same error text, and an omitted kind still defaults to agent instead of being rejected, which is asserted separately because it sits outside the vocabulary correspondence.\n\nGates at this exact head: go vet silent, go build ./... clean, internal/app and internal/workroom pass, both pass under -race, the full suite passes, git diff --check clean against main, worktree clean, three ahead of main and zero behind.\n\nArchitecture: internal/workroom and internal/app. The layer contract is preserved and slightly sharpened — the fold owns the roster vocabulary and the application consumes it through one exported predicate, which is the direction docs/reference/architecture.md already describes. No boundary moves, so that page does not change.\n\nSecurity: this touches an authority precondition, so I checked the direction of failure rather than only the result. validateAuthorityRole rejects exactly what it rejected before, because the predicate body is unchanged and only its name moved. A wrongly wide classifier would refuse a legitimate authority grant, which fails closed; a wrongly narrow one would admit a kind word as an authority, which is inert because nothing tests hasRole for agent, human or service. AddActor gains no reach it did not have.\n\nSimplification: this is the simplification, and it completes the one your seam started. I looked for more while I was in the file and found nothing worth taking without broadening the change, which you asked me not to do.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3526dd29f2422aa4d8ba905046495dbb9ed18352","branch":"request/legacy-roster-normalization","head":"a7fab5363793aac38c780aec7fc1f3236832cd1f","status":"ready-for-review"}}